TM plugin TODO
==============

Import an arbitrary file into local TM
---------------------------------------
The plumbing already exists but nothing in the UI calls it: remotetm.py
(which localtm inherits from) has upload_store(store_controller), which
pushes a whole store into the TM database via tmclient.upload_store() ->
tmserver.py's PUT /store/{sid} -> factory.getobject() - so it already
accepts anything translate-toolkit can parse, TMX included. Only
push_store() (auto-triggered on saving the *current* file) is wired to
anything; upload_store() is dead from a UI standpoint.

Add a real UI action - a file-chooser dialog, roughly "Import into
Translation Memory..." - that runs the chosen file through
factory.getobject() and pushes it into localtm via that same path,
without needing the file open as the current document.

This is the practical way to help users migrating from other CAT tools,
Poedit in particular: Poedit's own TM is a Lucene index (see
src/tm/transmem.cpp upstream) - not worth reading directly from Python,
but Poedit can export it to TMX (src/tm/tmx_io.cpp upstream), and this
action would let a user bring that straight in. Not Poedit-specific
though - works for any TMX/PO/XLIFF a user points it at.

Re-validate migration.py against upstream releases
----------------------------------------------------
virtaal/plugins/migration.py's Lokalize import broke silently over time
purely from KDE/Lokalize moving on - wrong config/TM paths (pre-KDE4
~/.kde/... locations, superseded by XDG base dirs ~2014) and a schema
that had since been normalized across multiple tables. Worth periodically
re-checking its assumptions (paths, file formats, DB schemas) against
each app's current source, the same way this got fixed:
  - Poedit: https://github.com/vslavik/poedit (src/tm/*)
  - Lokalize: https://invent.kde.org/sdk/lokalize (src/tm/*)
  - KBabel: dead since ~2008, superseded by Lokalize in KDE4 - nothing to
    check, just confirm it's still dead.

No fixed cadence agreed yet - these apps don't release often enough to
need checking every virtaal release, but drift silently enough (no
loud failure, just a plugin that quietly migrates nothing) that it's
worth a deliberate look now and then rather than waiting for a bug
report.
